## Backend Architecture

Clean Architecture with DDD. Each module follows: `domain/ → application/ → infrastructure/ → presentation/`

**Modules:** admin, assets, auth, common, messaging, profile, tenants, users

### Module Shapes

Not every module has all four layers. When creating a new module, copy the
full four-layer exemplars:

- **auth, tenants, users** — full `domain/application/infrastructure/presentation`
  modules; the exemplars to copy for new modules.
- **admin, profile** — thin presentation-only facades (routers/endpoints only).
- **assets** — `domain` + `infrastructure` storage adapter (no HTTP surface).
- **messaging** — `application` + `infrastructure` scaffolding, plus a thin
  `domain/services` email interface and `templates/`; no presentation layer.
- **common** — shared kernel (settings, database, base classes, DI).

**CRITICAL:** all SQLAlchemy ORM models live centrally in
`backend/src/common/database/models/` — NOT per-module. Domain entities live in
each module's `domain/`; persistence models always go in `common`.

Domain models for concepts shared across modules (user, tenant, tenant_user,
roles, invitations) also live centrally, in `backend/src/common/domain/models/`;
feature modules own their repositories, use cases, and presentation. The layer
direction inside every module is enforced by import-linter
(`just backend lint-imports`, part of `just backend quality` and CI).

### Key Patterns
- **Use cases** are dataclasses implementing `UseCase` with an `execute()` method
- **Repositories** are abstract classes in `domain/`, implemented as SQL repos in `infrastructure/`
- **Presenters** convert domain entities to API response dicts
- **Routers** use `add_api_route()` to register endpoints
- **Exceptions** extend `DomainError` with code, message, and status_code
- **Database** uses async SQLAlchemy with Alembic migrations
- **Assets** use storage adapters for local/object storage
- **Directus** remains available as the internal admin console

### Common Commands

```bash
# Development (commands are grouped by area: backend / frontend / docs)
just backend dev              # Start API + Postgres + Redis + MinIO + Mailpit + Directus
just frontend dev             # Start frontend dev server
just docs dev                 # Start React Router 7 + Fumadocs docs dev server
just backend migrate          # Run DB migrations
just backend new-migration "name"  # Create new migration
just backend                  # List all backend commands

# Docker
just stop-all                 # Stop all containers
just backend build-prod       # Build production backend image
```

### Verify Your Changes

```bash
just backend test             # Backend tests; marker: unit (default) | single | api | all
just backend quality          # Backend format + lint + typecheck
pnpm -C frontend verify       # Frontend type-check + biome + vitest + architecture boundaries + build
lefthook install              # Optional: enable the pre-commit hooks from lefthook.yml
```

Backend commands run through docker compose and need `backend/.env`; the just
recipes create it from `backend/.env.example` automatically on first run.

### Backend Entry Points
- API: `backend/config/main.py` → port 8200
- Async task worker: `backend/config/tasks.py` via SAQ
- Scripts: `backend/scripts/seed_users.py`, `backend/scripts/bootstrap_tenant_roles.py`

### Database
- PostgreSQL
- Migrations: `backend/src/common/database/versions/`
- Initial migration: `20260707_000001_initial_[[ python_name ]].py`

## Frontend

The frontend architecture source of truth is
`docs/internal/architecture/frontend-architecture.md`.

Before modifying `frontend/`, read that document and follow it over older
layer-based guidance. The target structure is feature-first:

```text
frontend/src/app -> frontend/src/features -> frontend/src/entities -> frontend/src/shared
```

Do not reintroduce the retired frontend architecture
`domain/application/infrastructure/presentation`. Browser code must call the
backend only through same-origin Next.js BFF routes (`/api/...`) using the
HTTP helpers described in the architecture document.

## Agent Skills

`.claude/skills/` is the canonical skills directory. `.codex/skills/`,
`.opencode/skills/`, and `.agents/skills/` are synced subsets of it — never
edit those copies directly. Fix drift with `just sync-skills`; drift fails the
pre-commit hook and CI via `python3 scripts/sync_skills.py --check`.

## Copier Template

*Canonical boilerplate repo only — generated projects do not include this
machinery (nor this concern).*

The public Copier template (`Llamitai/[[ project_slug ]]-template`) is 100%
generated from this repo by `scripts/build_template.py` — never edited by
hand. `scripts/branding_tokens.json` is the single source for branding tokens
and exclusions, shared with the `rename-project` skill; when you introduce new
branding, add it there (the `template` job in CI fails on leftovers
otherwise). `template/copier.yml` holds the questionnaire;
`.github/workflows/publish-template.yml` publishes the mirror on `v*` tags.
Validate locally with `just template check`. One-time setup and release
runbook: `docs/internal/template-publishing.md`.

## Optional Local Tooling

Agent configs reference these local binaries; each is optional and its absence
is non-fatal:

- **codegraph** — MCP server exposing a code knowledge graph; needs a one-time
  `codegraph init` per project.
- **pyright-langserver** — Python language server used by OpenCode LSP.
- **typescript-language-server** — TypeScript language server used by OpenCode LSP.

## Style Guidelines
- Backend: Python, snake_case, type hints, async/await
- Frontend: TypeScript, camelCase, functional components
- API responses use camelCase (presenters convert from snake_case)
- Tests and fixtures must use [[ project_name ]] examples only
- Commit messages in English
- Language policy: agent/instruction files and code are in English;
  `docs/content/` and `docs/internal/` are authored in Spanish; module READMEs
  (`backend/`, `frontend/`, `docs/internal/`) are Spanish by convention
